  "query_preview": "..Light winds and a dry air mass are leading to some\nof the coldest temperatures so far this fall across much of\nnorthern Arizona. Temperatures are already in the teens at the\nusual high country cold spots as of 2 AM, this includes Window \nRock, Tusayan, Flagstaff, and here at the NWS office in Bellemont \nwhere it is 17 degrees. After the cold start, expect a dry day \nwith passing high clouds and light southerly breezes. Highs will \nbe slightly below average for late November. On Monday, a weak \ntrough will clip the four corners, bringing a chance for rain and \nsnow showers (40-60%) to far northern Apache and Navajo counties. \nSnow levels will be 5000-6000 feet with a dusting at lower \nelevations up to around an inch for the highest parts of the \nChuska Mountains. The rest of northern Arizona should remain dry, \nwith winds gradually shifting to northerly as the system passes \nby.\n\nTuesday through Saturday - weak high pressure over the region\nTuesday with a continuation of cool and dry weather. A trough\nmoves through on Wednesday, but model trends continue to favor dry\nweather and just some high clouds with this one. For Thanksgiving\ninto the weekend - latest ensemble forecasts favor ridging over\nthe Southwest on Thanksgiving continuing through at least\nSaturday. Current forecasts are for dry weather and daytime\ntemperatures warming to several degrees above average by the\nweekend.",
